      "claim": "A close donepezil analogue with the same 5,6-dimethoxy-indanone and N-(3-methylpyridin-2-ylmethyl)piperidine core was reported to penetrate the BBB, supporting CNS access for the query scaffold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "Donepezil N-oxide, a direct metabolite of the query, achieved a brain-to-plasma concentration ratio of 1.80 in rats, demonstrating meaningful net brain exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "A close donepezil analogue showed brain/plasma ratios of 4.2-8.5 in mice after intragastric dosing, indicating excellent BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "Donepezil N-oxide was detected in rat brain by LC-MS/MS, directly showing that the donepezil scaffold can cross the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "A benzylpiperidine-containing iodobenzamide accumulated significantly in monkey brain, providing cross-species evidence for BBB penetration of this chemotype.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C05"],
      "claim": "A 1-benzyl-4-piperidinyl benzamide showed significant and lasting high brain uptake in mice, supporting CNS access for the benzylpiperidine motif.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "[11C]MC113, a dimethoxy-substituted CNS ligand, showed high brain uptake in mice by microPET, supporting brain penetration of this scaffold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "[11C]MC80, a dimethoxy-substituted benzylpiperidine analogue, showed high initial brain uptake in mice, supporting CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "[11C]SA4503 showed high brain uptake in rats, with 75-85% receptor-specific binding, supporting meaningful CNS exposure for this structural family.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "In vivo studies demonstrated high and receptor-specific brain uptake of [11C]SA4503, supporting CNS access for this structural family.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "An 18F-labeled benzylpiperidine benzamide analogue showed high initial brain uptake in rats, supporting rapid BBB crossing.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "N-(N-benzylpiperidin-4-yl)-2-[18F]fluorobenzamide showed good brain uptake in mice, supporting CNS penetration of the benzylpiperidine amide class.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Dexetimide, a benzylpiperidine-containing radiotracer, is described as having good brain penetration, supporting CNS access for this chemotype.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "A brain-permeable 6,7-dimethoxy-4-oxo-4H-chromene hybrid with a benzylpiperidine side chain was evaluated as a neurogenic agent, supporting CNS access for related scaffolds.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "A seleno-donepezil analogue was able to penetrate the CNS in an in vitro BBB model, supporting brain penetration for this structural family.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C16"],
      "claim": "An in vitro blood-brain barrier model indicated that a seleno-donepezil analogue can penetrate the CNS, providing additional support for this scaffold family.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "In silico ADME evaluation of PQM130, a benzylpiperidine hybrid, predicted good human absorption and BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C20"],
      "claim": "SwissADME predicted a close donepezil analogue to be a non-substrate of P-glycoprotein, suggesting that BBB absorption for that analogue is not compromised by this efflux transporter.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C19"],
      "claim": "A benzylpiperidine sulfonamide analogue was predicted by QikProp to have a CNS status of +, supporting CNS penetration despite a modest log BB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C21"],
      "claim": "The des-benzyl fragment of donepezil had a low Kp brain of 0.19 in rats, indicating that removal of the benzylpiperidine moiety impairs brain penetration; this is only partially transferable to the intact query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C12"],
      "claim": "SwissADME predicted a thiazole-fused donepezil analogue to be a P-glycoprotein substrate, suggesting possible efflux compromise; however, this is in silico and for a distant analogue.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "In silico prediction for a more rigid thiazole-fused donepezil analogue suggested non-BBB permeation, but this is a distant analogue and in silico only.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
